# ─── Environment + raw signer keys ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ENV selects the deploy environment. It is normalised at load (trimmed,
# lower-cased, and "prod" is treated as "production"), so "Production",
# " production ", "PROD" etc. all count. Anything else (including unset,
# which defaults to "development") is non-production.
ENV=development
#
# Several signers (IT operator, gasless relayer, ramp gas sponsor, PM release
# local signer) are, today, a raw private key read from an env var. That is a
# dev/staging convenience only: in production it is REFUSED by default and
# the corresponding feature stays dormant (IT + utility bills, gasless
# relaying, ramp gas sponsorship, PM auto-settle, respectively) rather than
# running with a hot key that isn't in a proper key store.
#
# ALLOW_RAW_SIGNER_KEYS=true is the interim opt-in until a KMS-backed signer
# ships (see internal/services/release_signer.go — "kms" is not yet
# implemented). Setting it logs `ALERT raw_signer_key_in_production=1
# key=<NAME>` at boot for every raw key actually used, one line per key, so
# the exposure is visible in every log sink without ever logging the key
# itself.
#
# IMPORTANT — rollout: do not flip ENV to "production" on its own. Today's
# production process runs with ENV NOT EXACTLY "production" (case identified
# 2026-09), which is what currently keeps IT, utility bills, and any raw
# signer key alive. Setting ENV=production without ALLOW_RAW_SIGNER_KEYS=true
# will silently take IT, bills, gasless relaying, ramp gas sponsorship and PM
# auto-settle dormant. Set both together in the same deploy:
#   ENV=production
#   ALLOW_RAW_SIGNER_KEYS=true
# Before that flip, also review: gin is switched to ReleaseMode under
# ENV=production (quieter logs, different default error responses), and the
# nil-chain-client fallbacks in escrow_service.go, mcgp_swap_service.go,
# otc_service.go and service_contact_service.go start refusing to build a
# partial unsigned tx once ENV=production (they already do this correctly for
# a broken chain client; the concern is only environments that route through
# them without a chain client on purpose, e.g. certain test/dev tooling).
ALLOW_RAW_SIGNER_KEYS=false

# Flutterwave v4 — the platform's fiat payment gateway (Nomba declined
# production API access, 2026-07). Several pieces of IT depend on this:
# withdrawal FX quotes, per-user fiat virtual accounts, PM sell payouts, the
# webhook route, and the brokered stablecoin ramp below.

# FLUTTERWAVE_BASE_URL defaults to Flutterwave's SANDBOX API. Leaving this
# blank is NOT the same as leaving it unset: getEnv (config.go) uses
# os.LookupEnv, so a bare `FLUTTERWAVE_BASE_URL=` line still counts as "set"
# (to ""), which defeats this compiled default — the client
# (flutterwave_client.go, fwDefaultSandboxBase) then re-defaults it back to
# sandbox anyway, silently, with no warning log. PRODUCTION MUST set this
# explicitly to Flutterwave's production base URL. Do not invent that URL
# here: it was mid-migration on Flutterwave's side as of 2026-08 and must be
# confirmed with Flutterwave at go-live, not guessed.
FLUTTERWAVE_BASE_URL=https://developersandbox-api.flutterwave.com
# Environment-neutral — same IDP host for sandbox and production.
FLUTTERWAVE_IDP_URL=https://idp.flutterwave.com
# Absent client id/secret ⇒ NewFlutterwaveClient returns nil and the whole FW
# client is dormant: withdrawal FX quotes, virtual accounts, and PM sell
# payouts all error until both are set.
FLUTTERWAVE_CLIENT_ID=
FLUTTERWAVE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Absent ⇒ the Flutterwave webhook route stays dormant (main.go only
# registers it once this is set).
FLUTTERWAVE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Absent ⇒ the brokered stablecoin ramp below stays dormant, regardless of
# FW_STABLE_DEPOSIT_ADDRESS_POLYGON.
FLUTTERWAVE_MERCHANT_ID=
# Optional — parents every Flutterwave virtual account. Left blank, one is
# minted lazily on boot and persisted per environment, so operators needn't
# dig it out of the dashboard by hand.
FLUTTERWAVE_CUSTOMER_ID=
FLUTTERWAVE_COMPANY_NAME=TSA Connect
FLUTTERWAVE_COMPANY_EMAIL=payments@mcgpchain.com
FLUTTERWAVE_COMPANY_PHONE=

# ─── Central-account inbound attribution ────────────────────────────────────
#
# INBOUND_MODE selects where payers are sent. "va" is the legacy behaviour: a
# per-user Flutterwave virtual account, attributed by the charge webhook.
# "central" routes every payer to the one company account below, where
# attribution instead comes from polling the wallet statement and matching the
# sender's name against their Didit-verified legal name.
#
# It is a single global flag on purpose. A per-user rollout would put two live
# attribution paths over one account, and a credit that both or neither claims.
#
# Flipping it does NOT close the virtual accounts already minted: nothing
# deactivates them at Flutterwave, and the charge webhook still credits them, so
# a payer who saved an old account number keeps being credited that way. Central
# mode only stops new accounts being minted and stops the app showing the old
# ones. Retiring them at the provider is a separate, deliberate exercise.
# Reverting is this variable plus a restart.
INBOUND_MODE=va

# The company account every payer is sent to in central mode. REQUIRED when
# INBOUND_MODE=central — boot refuses to start without all three, because a
# pay-into screen with a blank account number is a user transferring money
# nowhere. The number is checked as a 10-digit NUBAN; that cannot catch a
# transposed digit, only the bank can, so check it against the dashboard.
INBOUND_CENTRAL_ACCOUNT_NUMBER=
INBOUND_CENTRAL_BANK_NAME=
INBOUND_CENTRAL_ACCOUNT_NAME=

# Wallet funding fires NO webhook, so this poll is the only way an inbound
# credit is ever noticed, and the interval is the payer's wait. Also required
# in central mode: boot refuses without Flutterwave credentials, since without
# them nothing reads the account at all.
INBOUND_POLL_INTERVAL=30s
# Min gap between a finished statement poll and one triggered by a user tapping "I've sent the transfer" (global, not per user).
INBOUND_CHECK_COOLDOWN=10s
# How far back each poll reads. Overlap is free — ingestion is idempotent on the
# NIBSS session id — whereas a window shorter than an outage silently loses every
# credit that arrived during it. Deliberately far wider than the interval.
INBOUND_STATEMENT_LOOKBACK=24h

# Operators alerted on every held credit. Comma-separated. A held credit is
# customer money nobody can be shown to own, and by design nothing releases it
# automatically: no expiry, no auto-refund. Left blank, holds still queue at
# /api/admin/inbound/credits but nobody is paged, and the queue is only found by
# someone choosing to open it.
# The same list is also emailed when a money-path background job (inbound poller
# and reconciler, IT sell/buy/stable workers, bill reconciler, P2P chain
# reconciler, PM recovery, settlement forward sweep, Paycrest poller, ramp
# conversion) fails every run for ~5 minutes — hourly while it stays down, and
# once when it recovers. Blank leaves the `[jobs] ALERT worker_failing_total=1`
# log line as the only signal.
INBOUND_ALERT_EMAILS=

# Stablecoin ⇄ fiat ramp (brokered through Flutterwave; no float is held)
IT_STABLE_SPREAD_BPS=155
IT_STABLE_RATE_LOCK_SECONDS=900
# Flutterwave's own Polygon deposit address — dashboard-only, copied by hand.
FW_STABLE_DEPOSIT_ADDRESS_POLYGON=
# How far a deposit may exceed the amount it was quoted for and still pay out
# automatically. Beyond it the sell is parked for an operator: the payout scales
# with what actually arrived, so an unbounded ratio would let a small quote
# claim an arbitrarily large deposit.
IT_STABLE_OVER_DELIVERY_TOL_BPS=200
# Provider floor for a sell, in major units. Flutterwave rejects a conversion
# below its own minimum AFTER the user has irreversibly sent funds, so the quote
# refuses first — the last point where refusing is still free. The app enforces
# the same number, but that is a usability guard: a client-side check does not
# protect a direct API caller. Leaving this blank or malformed keeps the built-in
# default; the floor can be raised or lowered, never removed.
IT_STABLE_MIN_SELL=10

# ── IT sell recovery: trust "no transfer at Flutterwave" ──
# The sell reconciler and the admin sell actions look payouts up by reference
# in Flutterwave's GET /transfers listing. When that listing says a reference
# has no transfer, true lets them act on it: the reconciler re-sends a payout
# that was already attempted (same reference) and reverses a sell whose retry
# was refused, and an admin may reverse or retry-payout such a sell.
# Default false. The listing's response shape was built from Flutterwave's
# published v4 schema and has NOT been confirmed against the live API; a
# misread "absent" pays the user twice (naira sent AND MCGP returned). While
# false those sells only ALERT and appear in GET /api/it/admin/sell/stuck.
# Provider-confirmed outcomes (a transfer found SUCCESSFUL/FAILED) and first
# sends of payouts that were provably never sent work either way.
IT_SELL_FW_ABSENCE_TRUSTED=false

# ── Ramp gas sponsorship (Paycrest sells on base/arbitrum/polygon/bsc pay no gas) ──
# Off unless both are set. The key must NOT be the relayer, IT operator or PM release key.
RAMP_GAS_SPONSORSHIP=false
RAMP_SPONSOR_PRIVATE_KEY=
RAMP_SPONSOR_CHAINS=base,arbitrum,polygon,bsc
# Gas units per UTC day, per chain. A runaway-bug backstop (~4k relays/day at
# ~120k gas each); the balance floor and float alert protect the money.
RAMP_SPONSOR_DAILY_GAS_BUDGET=500000000
# Gas top-ups a user may leave UNUSED (order expired/cancelled/failed with
# nothing paid) in a rolling 24h. Top-ups that led to a deposit never count.
RAMP_SPONSOR_TOPUP_UNUSED_DAILY_CAP=2
# Per-chain overrides (wei). Defaults: floor ~$5, max single top-up ~$0.10-0.30.
# RAMP_SPONSOR_BALANCE_FLOOR_WEI_BASE=2000000000000000
# RAMP_SPONSOR_TOPUP_MAX_WEI_BSC=500000000000000

# ── Ramp treasury / converted buys (Paycrest → our treasury → LiFi → user's own chain/token) ──
# Off unless both are set. The key must NOT be the relayer, IT operator, PM
# release, or ramp sponsor key — the treasury holds USER FUNDS in transit, so
# sharing its key with any other hot signer is refused at boot.
RAMP_CONVERSION_BUY=false
RAMP_TREASURY_PRIVATE_KEY=
RAMP_TREASURY_CHAINS=base,arbitrum,polygon,bsc,optimism,avalanche,linea,sonic,ethereum
# Paycrest's own onramp cap per order, and this chain's total in-flight
# exposure across every converted buy the treasury hasn't yet settled.
RAMP_TREASURY_MAX_INFLIGHT_USD_PER_ORDER=2000
RAMP_TREASURY_MAX_INFLIGHT_USD_PER_CHAIN=10000
# Per-chain low-balance warning (wei): below it a lane logs a refill ALERT.
# Not a stop — a lane keeps working while it can pay for the transaction in
# hand, and converted routes stop being OFFERED once it cannot. Defaults are
# placeholders around one bridge tx (~$5-10 of native gas); retune once real
# bridge gas costs are observed in production.
# RAMP_TREASURY_NATIVE_FLOOR_WEI_BASE=5000000000000000
# RAMP_TREASURY_NATIVE_FLOOR_WEI_ETHEREUM=10000000000000000
# Contracts a LiFi route may make the treasury call or approve. Defaults pin
# LiFi's diamond per chain (read from li.quest/v1/chains; Linea has its own).
# Override only when LiFi migrates a diamond — comma-separated. A chain whose
# list is empty or holds a malformed address gets no lane at boot.
# RAMP_TREASURY_ROUTERS_BSC=0x1231DEB6f5749EF6cE6943a275A1D3E7486F4EaE
# Decision 10: how long a converted buy may sit with no broadcast bridge
# attempt before it is refunded instead of retried again.
RAMP_CONVERSION_RETRY_MINUTES=30
# Decision 6: smallest converted-buy order, measured on the settlement leg's
# stablecoin amount. Ethereum/Linea override higher — their gas cost makes a
# $1 order not worth converting.
RAMP_CONVERSION_MIN_USD=1
RAMP_CONVERSION_MIN_USD_ETHEREUM=5
RAMP_CONVERSION_MIN_USD_LINEA=25
# ConversionOrchestrator tunables — shown at their code defaults (6h stall
# window, 24h hard leg ceiling, $50 automatic top-up ceiling). Override to
# retune without a redeploy.
# RAMP_CONVERSION_STALL_MINUTES=360
# RAMP_CONVERSION_LEG_CEILING_HOURS=24
# RAMP_CONVERSION_MAX_SHORTFALL_USD=50
# F — the one user-facing markup, in percent. Priced into every quoted rate
# and sent to Paycrest as the sender fee on direct orders. Production: 1.
PAYCREST_SENDER_FEE_PERCENT=1
# B, in bps — the part of F that pays a conversion's round trip (LiFi + our
# gas). Converted orders send Paycrest F − B. Conversion is refused unless
# 0 < B < F. What the round trip does not spend is kept.
RAMP_ROUND_TRIP_BUDGET_BPS=50

# ── LiFi swap aggregator (AllSwap, ramp-conversion treasury, IT stable verifier) ──
# API key is optional — without it requests hit LiFi's anonymous, rate-limited
# tier. Integrator + fee are also optional; setting both collects a percentage
# cut, which requires a matching registration at portal.li.fi with a fee
# wallet configured.
LIFI_API_KEY=
LIFI_INTEGRATOR_ID=
LIFI_INTEGRATOR_FEE=
# LIFI_BASE_URL overrides LiFi's public API base (defaults to
# https://li.quest/v1) for every LiFi client in the backend. It exists solely
# so a LOCAL REHEARSAL can point at a stand-in server: the real LiFi API can
# never satisfy a status check for a converted buy whose transaction only
# exists on a local chain fork. LEAVE UNSET IN PRODUCTION. A blank or
# whitespace-only value falls back to the default rather than being used
# verbatim.
LIFI_BASE_URL=
